    <!-- Section 1: Scale -->
    <section id="scale" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">1. The Scale: Numbers That Don't Seem Real</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Start with a simple fact to understand the rest of this article: <strong>Anping County's wire mesh industry generates tens of billions of RMB in annual output, employs over 100,000 people, and ships product to more than 190 countries.</strong> The county's industrial parks host over 500 registered wire mesh manufacturers, alongside thousands of smaller workshops and family operations.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Anping's stainless steel wire mesh, welded wire mesh, and filter cloth are used in oil refineries in Texas, pharmaceutical plants in Germany, mines in Chile, and construction sites in Dubai. When a distributor in Rotterdam or Chicago quotes you "Chinese wire mesh," the probability it was woven, welded, or galvanized in Anping is overwhelming.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Why This Matters for Buyers</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          Cluster concentration creates a buyer's market. Because hundreds of factories compete within a 30-kilometer radius, prices are 15–40% lower than equivalent product from dispersed manufacturing regions — and the intense competition forces continuous quality improvement at the top tier. The challenge isn't finding a supplier in Anping. It's finding the <em>right tier</em> of supplier for your application.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: History -->
    <section id="history" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">2. How Anping Became the Wire Mesh Capital</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Anping's dominance wasn't built in a decade — it was built over five centuries, then industrialized in one.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>The pre-industrial era (1500s–1949).</strong> Historical records show wire weaving in Anping dating to the Ming Dynasty. Local artisans produced silk and metal mesh for sieves, screens, and grain sorting. The region had two advantages: proximity to iron production in Hebei, and a weaving craft tradition passed down through generations of families. By the early 20th century, Anping weavers were producing wire mesh on wooden looms for regional markets.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>The mechanization era (1950s–1990s).</strong> After 1949, Anping's weaving workshops were gradually mechanized. Wire drawing machines replaced hand-pulled wire; metal looms replaced wooden frames. The critical development was vertical integration: instead of importing wire, Anping factories began drawing their own from steel rod — first hot-dipped galvanized wire, then stainless steel. By the 1980s reform era, township and village enterprises were exporting through state trading companies.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>The export boom (1990s–present).</strong> Three forces converged: China joined the WTO in 2001, slashing export friction; Anping built dedicated industrial parks with shared infrastructure (galvanizing lines, testing labs, logistics hubs); and the internet let small Anping factories reach global buyers directly. The cluster achieved critical mass — every input (wire rod, loom parts, acid, zinc, packing materials) became available locally, at the lowest prices in the world.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">The Cluster Effect, Explained</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary">
          A wire mesh factory anywhere else must source wire from one supplier, looms from another, galvanizing from a third, and pay to ship everything between them. In Anping, a factory can source drawn wire in the morning, send it to a neighboring galvanizer by lunch, and pack finished rolls for export by evening. This density is why Anping's cost structure is unbeatable — and why attempts to replicate the cluster elsewhere have failed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

