    <!-- Section 5: Sourcing -->
    <section id="sourcing" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">5. How to Source From an Anping Wire Mesh Factory</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Sourcing from Anping follows a repeatable process. Skipping steps is where most bad experiences originate:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6 mb-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Step 1: Define Your Spec Before You Contact Anyone</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Anping factories respond to precise specifications, not vague descriptions. "I need stainless mesh" gets you a Tier 3 quote for 201-grade material. "316L stainless, 100 mesh, 0.10mm wire, plain weave, 1.2m × 30m rolls, ASTM E11 tolerance" gets you comparable quotes from qualified factories. Use our <a href="/tools/inquiry-template-generator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">inquiry template generator</a> to format your request.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Step 2: Shortlist 3–5 Factories</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Don't contact one factory and hope. Contact several, compare response quality, ask for factory registration documents and recent export records. Factories that respond with technical questions ("What pressure will this see? What fluid?") are almost always better than factories that respond with only a price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Step 3: Order Samples Before Production</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Always order paid samples (typically $20–100 including shipping) and test them: mesh count with a counter, wire diameter with calipers, material with a magnet test (300-series stainless is essentially non-magnetic) or XRF if available. A $50 sample test prevents a $5,000 mistake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Step 4: Negotiate Terms, Not Just Price</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Standard Anping export terms: 30% T/T deposit, 70% against B/L copy. For first orders under $10,000, consider Trade Assurance or an L/C. Negotiate tolerance clauses into the PO — "mesh count tolerance ±2%, wire diameter tolerance ±0.01mm" — so disputes have an objective basis. MOQs are flexible; a factory quoting 500m MOQ on standard mesh will often accept 200m at a small price premium.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Step 5: Inspect Before Shipment (For Orders >$5,000)</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Third-party inspection (SGS, Bureau Veritas, TÜV, or local Anping inspection agents at $150–300/day) is cheap insurance. Specify inspection criteria in the PO: mesh count, wire diameter, material grade verification, roll length/width, packaging integrity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        You can browse verified producers in our <a href="/factories/" class="text-theme-primary underline">Anping factory directory</a>, which lists capabilities, certifications, and export experience for each listed manufacturer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: Verification -->
    <section id="verification" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">6. Verification: Separating Factories From Trading Companies</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        One of Anping's quirks: many "factories" you find on Alibaba and Google are trading companies — middlemen who take your order, subcontract it to a workshop, and mark it up 15–30%. Trading companies aren't inherently bad (good ones provide quality control and communication value), but you should know what you're dealing with. Here's how to verify:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Verification Method</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">What It Reveals</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Cost / Difficulty</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">Business license check (National Enterprise Credit System)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Registered business scope — "manufacturing" vs "trading"</td>
              <td class="p-3">Free / Easy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">Video factory tour (live, not pre-recorded)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Loom count, actual production, workshop condition</td>
              <td class="p-3">Free / Easy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">Request mill certificate for raw wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">Material traceability; real factories have them</td>
              <td class="p-3">Free / Easy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3">On-site visit or hired inspection</td>
              <td class="p-3">Everything — the gold standard</td>
              <td class="p-3">$150–500 / Moderate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3">Third-party PMI material testing (XRF)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Confirms 304/316 grade, detects 201 substitution</td>
              <td class="p-3">$200–500 per batch / Moderate</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Red Flags Specific to Anping</h3>
        <ul class="text-theme-secondary space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li>🚩 Refuses live video tour but sends polished videos (often stock footage)</li>
          <li>🚩 Business license lists "trade" or "commerce" scope, claims to be a factory</li>
          <li>🚩 Cannot explain their own production process in technical detail</li>
          <li>🚩 Quotes 304 stainless at prices below current nickel-influenced market rates</li>
          <li>🚩 Registered address is a residential building or office tower, not an industrial park</li>
          <li>🚩 Offers to ship "any mesh product" — real factories have specialties</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">7. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is "Anping wire mesh factory" just a marketing label?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            No — it's a geographic and industrial reality. Anping County is a specific jurisdiction in Hengshui, Hebei Province, and its wire mesh cluster is documented by Chinese government statistics and industry associations. However, some trading companies outside Anping use the name for credibility, which is why verification (business license address, factory tour) matters.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How much cheaper is Anping wire mesh compared to domestic (US/EU) suppliers?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For equivalent specification, expect 30–60% savings on standard products (welded mesh, galvanized fencing) and 15–30% on precision products (316L filter cloth, dutch weave) after freight and duties. The gap narrows for low-volume custom work and for Tier 1 precision manufacturers, whose prices approach Western levels but still undercut them on lead time and flexibility.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What's a typical minimum order quantity from an Anping factory?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Standard products: 1–5 rolls (30–100m each) for woven mesh; one full welded mesh panel bundle for construction mesh. Custom specifications: typically 100–500m depending on complexity. Tier 3 workshops may accept trial orders of a single roll; Tier 1 precision factories may require 500m+ for custom weaves. Everything is negotiable against total order value.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I know if I'm getting 304/316 stainless steel and not cheaper 201 grade?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Three layers of protection: (1) request the raw wire mill certificate with heat numbers matching your batch; (2) do a magnet test — 201 is strongly magnetic, 304/316 nearly non-magnetic (note: cold-worked 304 can show slight magnetism); (3) for orders over $10,000, pay $200–400 for third-party XRF/PMI testing. The nickel price difference between 201 and 304 is a red-flag detector — quotes far below market almost always mean substitution.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Should I visit Anping in person?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For orders above $50,000 annually, yes — it's one of the most buyer-friendly industrial regions in China. Anping is 250km south of Beijing (about 1.5 hours by high-speed rail to Hengshui station). Factories are accustomed to foreign visitors; serious ones will arrange pickup, factory tours, and sample demonstrations. A two-day visit can evaluate 5–8 factories. Alternatively, hire a local sourcing agent for $150–300/day who speaks the local dialect and knows which workshops are behind which storefronts.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Do Anping factories handle shipping and export documentation?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Tier 2 and Tier 1 factories handle everything: FOB Tianjin or Qingdao is standard (Anping is inland, ~3 hours from Tianjin port), CIF/CIP terms are common, and they prepare commercial invoices, packing lists, and certificates of origin routinely. Tier 3 workshops often cannot export directly and will route through an agent — another reason to verify tier before ordering.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

